org:iv-nilo	organization	NILO (NILO Limited)	NZ cleantech turning waste plastic into low-carbon adhesives for engineered wood	Auckland, New Zealand	https://www.nilo.net	{cleantech,"plastic recycling","circular economy",adhesives,"deep tech","Icehouse Portfolio"}	\N	{"bio": "NILO is an Auckland-based cleantech / deep tech company that upcycles hard-to-recycle polyethylene (PE) and polypropylene (PP) plastic waste into an industrial adhesive. The binder is positioned as a drop-in replacement for the urea formaldehyde used in engineered wood products such as particle board, MDF and plywood, cutting formaldehyde off-gassing and carbon emissions. In 2023 Inter IKEA Group took a 12.5% stake and a board seat, then co-led a NZ$13m Series A in December 2024 alongside Pacific Channel. The company says it had previously raised about NZ$15m from investors including Icehouse Ventures and Sir Stephen Tindall's K1W1.", "notes": "Disambiguation: this is the NZ cleantech \\"NILO Limited\\" (nilo.net / formerly nilo.world), not same-named foreign firms. Icehouse Ventures backing is firmly confirmed: NILO lists Icehouse Ventures on its own investors page and multiple media reports cite Icehouse as a pre-Series-A investor. Leadership note: the draft's CEO (Glen Willoughby) is out of date. Willoughby was CEO at the 2023 IKEA deal and Dec 2024 Series A, but NILO's current About page and a July 2025 article name Michael (Mike) Maunsell as CEO; Willoughby's LinkedIn now reads \\"Nilo Global.\\" Tim Williams remains Founder & Managing Director. One Dec-2024 press item loosely called Tim Williams \\"CEO,\\" which conflicts with the primary About-page titles. IKEA stake reported as 12.5% (IKEA/NILO press release; NZ Herald rounded to 12%). Funding figures are NZD. No co-founder beyond Tim Williams is independently named.", "stage": "Series A", "status": "active", "founded": "2018", "sources": ["https://www.nilo.net/", "https://www.nilo.net/about", "https://www.nilo.net/investors", "https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/ikea-takes-stake-in-kiwi-startup-nilo-the-story-behind-the-deal/IOPJBJS2TBHIJDXQGEGB63NHOI/", "https://www.pacificchannel.com/stories/pacific-channel-co-lead-13m-series-a-investment-alongside-inter-ikea-group-into-nilo-limited", "https://businessdesk.co.nz/article/news-in-brief/cleantech-company-nilo-secures-13m-in-series-a-fund-raise", "https://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/BU2507/S00144/nz-cleantech-company-nilo-hits-major-milestone-in-low-carbon-adhesive-made-from-plastic-waste.htm", "https://www.sustainableplastics.com/news/inter-ikea-purchases-stake-new-zealand-start", "https://www.linkedin.com/in/mikemaunsell/", "https://www.linkedin.com/in/tim-williams-3b947/"], "website": "https://www.nilo.net", "industry": "Cleantech / Advanced Materials", "confidence": "high", "verifyNote": "Confirmed: company identity, Auckland HQ, 2018 founding, the plastic-to-adhesive product, the NZ$13m Series A (Dec 2024, co-led by Inter IKEA and Pacific Channel), Inter IKEA's ~12.5% stake/board seat, and Icehouse Ventures as a backer (listed on NILO's own investors page). Corrected: removed Glen Willoughby as CEO — he is no longer NILO's CEO; NILO's About page and a July 2025 article name Michael (Mike) Maunsell as current CEO. Because Maunsell is CEO (not a founder) and the published bio for him is thin, only the verified founder, Tim Williams (Founder & Managing Director), is retained in the founders array; his bio and LinkedIn handle were independently confirmed. Could not independently confirm any co-founder beyond Tim Williams.", "statusDetail": "Active and operating; Inter IKEA Group holds a 12.5% stake with a board seat. Reported NZ$13m Series A (Dec 2024) co-led by Inter IKEA Group and Pacific Channel; ~NZ$15m raised prior to that round. In July 2025 announced a technical milestone for a furniture-grade sprayable adhesive.", "verifyVerdict": "corrected", "fundingNotable": "NZ$13m Series A (Dec 2024) co-led by Inter IKEA Group and Pacific Channel; Inter IKEA Group earlier acquired a ~12.5% stake (2023). Around NZ$15m raised before the Series A; named investors include Icehouse Ventures, K1W1 (Sir Stephen Tindall), SDG Impact Japan and the Clare Foundation.", "icehouseBacked": "confirmed"}	community:icehouse-ventures	\N	2026-05-31 08:57:29.572	2026-06-01 01:42:36.222	Startup
