org:aquila	organization	Aquila	Building an energy grid of light — wireless, long-range optical power-beaming systems.	Sydney, Australia	https://www.aquila.earth/	{"Space & Defence",Active,"Blackbird Portfolio","Wireless power transmission","Renewable energy"}	\N	{"kind": "portfolio_company", "sector": "Deep Tech", "status": "Active", "twitter": null, "website": "https://www.aquila.earth/", "linkedin": "https://au.linkedin.com/company/aquilaspace", "research": {"sources": ["https://www.blackbird.vc/portfolio/aquila", "https://www.aquila.earth/", "https://www.aquila.earth/about-us", "https://au.linkedin.com/company/aquilaspace", "https://www.startupdaily.net/topic/funding/blackbird-backs-energy-transmission-startup-aquila-earth-beams-up-3-million-seed-round-for-wireless-power-network/", "https://www.startupdaily.net/topic/funding/wireless-power-startup-aquila-charges-up-on-2-million-raise/", "https://www.startmate.com/writing/aquila-internet-of-energy-limitless-future-for-humanity", "https://www.linkedin.com/in/billy-jeremijenko-a31399183/", "https://nz.linkedin.com/in/nelson-smith", "https://www.crunchbase.com/organization/aquila-b1ec", "https://x.com/aquila_j"], "confidence": "high", "researchedAt": "2026-05-31", "missingFields": ["totalRaisedUsd", "twitter", "logoUrl"]}, "hqCountry": "Australia", "crunchbase": "https://www.crunchbase.com/organization/aquila-b1ec", "subSectors": ["Wireless power transmission", "Renewable energy", "Optical power beaming / photonics", "Drones / aerospace", "Space technology", "Energy infrastructure"], "exitDetails": null, "foundedYear": 2022, "statusGroup": "active", "totalRaised": "~A$5M (A$3M seed in 2023 + A$2M follow-on in 2024)", "fundingStage": "Seed", "snappedSector": "Space & Defence", "matchedCompany": "Aquila (Aquila Earth) — wireless optical power-beaming startup", "totalRaisedUsd": null, "longDescription": "Aquila (Aquila Earth) is an Australasian deep-tech startup building optical power-beaming technology that transmits electricity wirelessly via high-intensity light/laser beams instead of wires. Its Lightway product family begins with Solais, a system that wirelessly powers drones in flight by beaming optical energy from a ground transmitter to an onboard solar-cell receiver, and scales toward Earth-to-satellite energy networks (Bifrost, Helios, Indra). The long-term mission is to create an \\"internet of energy\\" — an infinitely scalable, space-based optical relay network that distributes renewable power from supply to demand anywhere on Earth. The company has attracted interest from customers including the US DARPA and defence partners."}	community:blackbird-ventures	\N	2026-06-01 01:42:34.43	2026-06-01 01:44:40.738	Portfolio Company
org:iv-sharesies	organization	Sharesies	NZ wealth platform giving anyone with $5 the same investing access as someone with $5m	Wellington, New Zealand	https://www.sharesies.nz	{fintech,investing,wealthtech,micro-investing,KiwiSaver,"Icehouse Portfolio"}	\N	{"bio": "Sharesies is a New Zealand wealth-development (fintech) platform that lets people invest small amounts in shares, ETFs, KiwiSaver and other products, including fractional shares. Founded in Wellington in 2017, it operates across New Zealand and Australia. By 2025/2026 the company reported around 930,000 to over 1,000,000 investors and more than NZ$12 billion in wealth managed on the platform. It is led by a three co-CEO (\\"3EO\\") structure made up of co-founders Brooke Roberts, Leighton Roberts and Sonya Williams.", "notes": "Six co-founders total (Brooke Roberts, Leighton Roberts, Sonya Williams, Richard Clark, Ben Crotty, Martyn Smith), all confirmed by Wikipedia and the Icehouse Ventures portfolio page. Only three (the two Robertses and Williams) are active executive leaders under the \\"3EO\\" co-CEO model; Richard Clark is the technology lead (CTO). LinkedIn URLs confirmed for the three co-CEOs; not found/confirmed for Clark, Crotty and Smith (left null). This is the Wellington, NZ fintech, distinct from any same-named foreign entity. Brooke and Leighton Roberts are married. ~NZ$500m valuation is as of the Oct 2021 Series C; no newer public valuation confirmed.", "stage": "Series C", "status": "active", "founded": "2017", "sources": ["https://www.icehouseventures.co.nz/companies/sharesies", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sharesies", "https://www.sharesies.nz/about", "https://www.ey.com/en_nz/entrepreneur-of-the-year/new-zealand/meet-our-2022-category-winners/sharesies-group", "https://businessdesk.co.nz/article/ceo-index/the-x-factor-behind-sharesies-leadership-success", "https://www.startupdaily.net/2021/10/kiwi-investment-platform-sharesies-raises-nz50-million-in-a-series-c/", "https://sharesies.com.au/blog/sharesies-group-raises-48-million-in-latest-funding-round", "https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/markets/sharesies-raises-25-million-to-expand-across-the-ditch/ASD6WJUULXMU47GEZLGHENSHLU/", "https://fintechnews.au/119988/new-zealand/4-fintech-startups-from-new-zealand-to-have-on-your-radar-in-2026/", "https://nz.linkedin.com/in/brooketanderson", "https://www.linkedin.com/in/leightonroberts/", "https://www.linkedin.com/in/sonya-williams-830ab747/"], "website": "https://www.sharesies.nz", "industry": "Fintech / Wealth & Investing", "confidence": "high", "verifyNote": "All major facts independently verified via fresh searches. Founders, the 3EO co-CEO structure, 2017 Wellington founding, active/independent status, ~NZ$500m Oct 2021 Series C (led by Amplo, with Icehouse Ventures) and the Dec 2020 NZ$25m round (Trade Me, Icehouse Ventures, K1W1) all confirmed. Icehouse backing confirmed on icehouseventures.co.nz (backed founders at the 2018 Showcase, plus the later rounds). The draft's \\"1M+ investors / NZ$12B+ assets by 2025\\" claim is supported by current 2025/2026 reporting (~930,000-1M+ investors, NZ$12B+ wealth); some 2024 sources (Wikipedia/Icehouse page) still cite the stale 700K-800K / NZ$5B figures, so the description was softened to a range. Minor refinements: Richard Clark recast as Co-founder & CTO, and founder bios tightened where prior education claims (e.g., a specific Brooke Roberts Stanford credential) could not be independently confirmed and were generalised. Confidence remains high.", "statusDetail": "Active and independent; valued at ~NZ$500m at its October 2021 Series C, with ~930,000-1M+ investors and NZ$12B+ in wealth managed reported by 2025/2026.", "verifyVerdict": "confirmed", "fundingNotable": "Raised NZ$25m in Dec 2020 (Trade Me, Icehouse Ventures, K1W1/Stephen Tindall) and a ~NZ$50m (A$48m) Series C in Oct 2021 led by US VC Amplo with Icehouse Ventures and others, valuing the company at ~NZ$500m.", "icehouseBacked": "confirmed"}	community:icehouse-ventures	\N	2026-05-31 08:57:29.572	2026-06-01 01:42:36.222	Startup
org:iv-vessev	organization	Vessev	Auckland maker of certified electric hydrofoiling passenger vessels	Auckland, New Zealand	https://www.vessev.com	{"electric vehicles",hydrofoil,maritime,cleantech,transportation,"Icehouse Portfolio"}	\N	{"bio": "Vessev is a New Zealand company that designs and builds electric hydrofoiling vessels and propulsion technology, lifting boats above the water to cut energy consumption by up to ~90%. Its flagship VS-9 launched in 2024 as what the company calls the world's first certified electric hydrofoiling vessel for commercial tourism, entering passenger service with Auckland ferry operator Fullers360 in January 2025. The company is expanding internationally (US office, a Northern Ireland resort customer) and is developing a larger VS-18 commuter ferry. It is headquartered in Auckland and backed by NZ and offshore venture investors.", "notes": "Auckland NZ electric-hydrofoil maritime company (vessev.com) — not to be confused with same-named foreign entities. Founding year is ambiguous across sources: Icehouse states 2018, some press credits Max Olson with starting work in 2017, and Blackbird's page indicates 2019; 2018 retained as Icehouse's stated figure. Eric Laakmann (CEO) joined ~2020, not a day-one founder — Max Olson is the original founder/CEO, now CTO. Olson's exact founding role at Halter is described variously (co-founder vs. first CTO; Craig Piggott is Halter's founder/CEO), so bio wording softened.", "stage": "Seed", "status": "active", "founded": "2018", "sources": ["https://www.icehouseventures.co.nz/companies/vessev", "https://www.vessev.com/our-story", "https://nz.linkedin.com/company/vessev", "https://www.blackbird.vc/portfolio/vessev", "https://aucklandeconomicdevelopment.com/invest/news/vessev-electric-hydrofoiling-propels-a-better-world", "https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/watch-vessevs-hydrofoiling-e-boat-takes-to-the-water-attracts-us-backer/AXARARBTYJGCHPGCK7R5MLNGQ4/", "https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/companies/freight-logistics/fullers-vessev-launch-worlds-first-electric-hydrofoiling-tourism-vessel-on-auckland-harbour/YSAY72UDZRDTLEPWDMRTZFCA5Q/", "https://electrek.co/2025/01/29/vessevs-vs-9-electric-hydrofoiling-boat-begins-passenger-operations-video/", "https://www.qantas.com/travelinsider/en/business/innovators/vessev.html", "https://pitchbook.com/profiles/company/234176-86", "https://www.crunchbase.com/organization/vessev"], "website": "https://www.vessev.com", "industry": "Maritime / Clean Transportation (electric marine vessels)", "confidence": "high", "verifyNote": "Independently confirmed via fresh searches: both founders (Max Olson — founder/original CEO, now CTO; Eric Laakmann — CEO, ex-Apple Watch, joined ~2020) verified across NZ Herald, Icehouse Ventures, Vessev and multiple outlets. Icehouse backing confirmed by its own portfolio page (Seed Fund II) and NZ Herald (~27% largest stake, Growth Fund II). Active status, VS-9/Fullers360 service, and investor list all confirmed. Corrections: softened the \\"co-founded Halter\\" claim (sources describe Olson as Halter's first CTO; Craig Piggott is founder/CEO); added Max Olson's LinkedIn URL surfaced in search; tightened oneLiner under 110 chars and removed the unverified Australia/Europe expansion phrasing from description/status (only US office and Northern Ireland customer are clearly sourced). Founding year remains ambiguous (2017/2018/2019); 2018 retained per Icehouse.", "statusDetail": "Active and scaling; VS-9 (\\"Kermadec\\") in commercial passenger service with Fullers360 (Auckland) since Jan 2025, US office opened 2025, first European customer (Finn Lough resort, Northern Ireland), VS-18 commuter ferry in development.", "verifyVerdict": "confirmed", "fundingNotable": "Backed by Icehouse Ventures (early/seed investor via Seed Fund II and largest shareholder, reported ~27% stake added to via its Growth Fund II), Blackbird Ventures, K1W1 (Sir Stephen Tindall), Shasta Ventures, GD1, and US-based Field Family Investments, plus a small Port of Auckland stake; also received ~NZ$1.2m in EECA Low Emission Transport Fund grants across three grants. PitchBook lists ~US$3.62m total disclosed; full round amounts not publicly confirmed.", "icehouseBacked": "confirmed"}	community:icehouse-ventures	\N	2026-05-31 08:57:29.572	2026-06-01 01:42:36.222	Startup
org:iv-halter	organization	Halter	Solar smart collars and software to virtually fence, remotely move and monitor cattle from a phone	Auckland, New Zealand	https://www.halterhq.com	{agritech,"virtual fencing",livestock,"IoT hardware","dairy/beef farming","Icehouse Portfolio"}	\N	{"bio": "Halter is a New Zealand agritech company that makes solar-powered, GPS-enabled smart collars plus farm-management software that let farmers virtually fence, remotely shift and proactively monitor cattle, using audio cues and vibration. Founded in 2016 by Craig Piggott (with co-founder Max Olson), it is headquartered in Auckland and has expanded into Australia and the United States, with an office in Boulder, Colorado. It reached unicorn status in 2025 with a NZ$1.65 billion (about US$1B) Series D. In March 2026 it raised a NZ$377 million (US$220M) Series E led by Founders Fund at a NZ$3.43 billion (US$2B) valuation, reporting roughly one million collars sold across 2,000+ farms and ranches.", "notes": "This is the NZ agritech Halter (halterhq.com), distinct from same-named foreign companies. Icehouse Ventures' own portfolio page names BOTH Craig Piggott and Max Olson as founders and states it first backed Piggott at 22 in 2016 via Seed Fund I — independently confirming both the co-founder claim and Icehouse backing. Max Olson left Halter and is now co-founder/CTO of Vessev. Valuation is reported in both NZD and USD; the company's own Series E release states NZ$3.43B / US$2B, though some NZ Herald headlines round to \\"NZ$3.3 billion.\\" Series D figures cited as US$100M / NZ$165M at ~NZ$1.65B (Wikipedia/Icehouse). Wikipedia does not name investors but does not contradict Icehouse backing.", "stage": "Series E", "status": "active", "founded": "2016", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halter_(company)", "https://www.icehouseventures.co.nz/companies/halter", "https://resources.icehouseventures.co.nz/blog/reflections-and-lessons-from-our-journey-with-halter", "https://www.halterhq.com/en-nz/news/halter-accelerates-international-expansion-following-seriese-fundraising-round", "https://www.caproasia.com/2026/03/27/new-zealand-beef-dairy-farm-technology-company-halter-raised-220-million-in-series-e-funding-at-2-billion-valuation-founded-in-2016-by-craig-piggott-investors-include-bond-bessemer-venture-part/", "https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/halter-raises-377m-at-33-billion-valuation-reveals-customer-numbers/premium/TQQEMUA52RCFHHSJHZF4KDTLUI/", "https://www.icehouseventures.co.nz/companies/vessev", "https://nz.linkedin.com/in/craig-piggott-9ab437aa", "https://nz.linkedin.com/in/max-olson-241471123"], "website": "https://www.halterhq.com", "industry": "Agritech (livestock management / virtual fencing)", "confidence": "high", "verifyNote": "Draft was substantially accurate. Independently confirmed: both founders (Piggott CEO, Olson first CTO, since left for Vessev) via Icehouse's portfolio page and Vessev/Blackbird sources; active status and 2025 unicorn milestone; Icehouse Ventures backing (Seed Fund I, 2016, ~200x return, ongoing investor); and the March 2026 Series E (NZ$377M/US$220M led by Founders Fund at NZ$3.43B/US$2B) via Halter's own release. Minor cleanups: shortened the oneLiner and trimmed the description; clarified the office as Boulder, Colorado; removed the unverifiable \\"Generate\\" from the named Series E investor list and the unsourced specific Series D LinkedIn/Vessev founding year. One discrepancy noted: NZ Herald headlines say \\"NZ$3.3B\\" while the company release and Caproasia say NZ$3.43B — kept the company figure.", "statusDetail": "Active and scaling internationally; unicorn since 2025. Operates in New Zealand, Australia and the US (offices in Auckland and Boulder, Colorado), with planned expansion into the UK, Ireland and South America.", "verifyVerdict": "confirmed", "fundingNotable": "Raised NZ$377M (~US$220M) Series E in March 2026 at a NZ$3.43B (~US$2B) valuation, led by Founders Fund; reached unicorn status in 2025 with a NZ$1.65B (~US$1B) Series D (US$100M / NZ$165M). Investors include Founders Fund, Blackbird, BOND, Bessemer Venture Partners, DCVC, NewView Capital, Promus Ventures, Ubiquity, and Icehouse Ventures (first backed Craig Piggott via Seed Fund I in 2016).", "icehouseBacked": "confirmed"}	community:icehouse-ventures	\N	2026-05-31 08:57:29.572	2026-06-01 01:42:36.222	Startup
org:iv-dawn-aerospace	organization	Dawn Aerospace	Space transportation company building reusable spaceplanes and green satellite propulsion systems.	Christchurch, New Zealand (and Delft, Netherlands)	https://www.dawnaerospace.com	{"space transportation","satellite propulsion",spaceplane,"deep tech","reusable launch","Icehouse Portfolio"}	\N	{"bio": "Dawn Aerospace is a space transportation company founded in 2017 that develops flight-proven green chemical satellite propulsion systems and a reusable, runway-launched suborbital spaceplane (the Mk-II Aurora). Its propulsion technology has flown on hundreds of thrusters in orbit, and on 12 November 2024 its Aurora spaceplane broke the sound barrier (Mach 1.1, ~82,000 ft), becoming the first New Zealand-designed and -manufactured aircraft to do so. The company runs a dual-headquarter model in Christchurch (NZ) and Delft (Netherlands) with operations across four countries and 120+ employees.", "notes": "Icehouse backing fully confirmed on Icehouse Ventures' own portfolio page: IV \\"backed Stefan and the founding team in 2018\\" (seed) and co-invested (~$10M, one of its largest NZ investments) in the US$20M Series A (Dec 2022) alongside GD1 and Movac. Headquarters is dual-sited (Christchurch NZ + Delft NL) with additional offices in France and the US. This is the NZ/Netherlands spaceplane and satellite-propulsion company founded by Stefan and James Powell — distinct from any same-named foreign entity. All five founders are independently confirmed by both Dawn's About page and IV's portfolio page. Stefan Powell holds CEO and CTO titles. Employee count cited as 120+ (Wikipedia, Aug 2025) to 130+ (company site). LinkedIn URLs for co-founders other than Stefan Powell were not confirmed, so left null.", "stage": "Series A", "status": "active", "founded": "2017", "sources": ["https://www.dawnaerospace.com/about", "https://www.icehouseventures.co.nz/companies/dawn-aerospace", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dawn_Aerospace", "https://www.nbr.co.nz/tech/dawn-aerospace-secures-20m-series-a/", "https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/companies/airlines/dawn-aerospace-gets-20m-boost-for-development-work/HVMPST73EBBBNJVT2VAIZGVCKA/", "https://www.dawnaerospace.com/latest-news/dawn-raises-20million", "https://payloadspace.com/dawn-20m-raise/", "https://theorg.com/org/dawn-aerospace/org-chart/stefan-powell", "https://www.linkedin.com/in/spowell111/"], "website": "https://www.dawnaerospace.com", "industry": "Aerospace / Space technology", "confidence": "high", "verifyNote": "Independently confirmed via Dawn's own About page, Icehouse Ventures' portfolio page, Wikipedia, and NZ business media. All five founders (Stefan Powell, Jeroen Wink, James Powell, Robert Werner, Tobias Knop) and their roles are correct; both Dawn and Icehouse list the identical five-person founding team. Icehouse backing confirmed (2018 seed + 2022 Series A co-investment). Status, US$20M Dec-2022 Series A at ~$170M valuation, $42M+ total funding, dual HQ, and the Nov 2024 Mach 1.1 milestone all verified. Minor refinements only: clarified Stefan also holds CTO title and noted the sound-barrier flight made Dawn the first NZ-designed AND -manufactured aircraft to go supersonic; confirmed Stefan's Rocket Lab/Hyperion background via The Org/Crunchbase. The draft was accurate.", "statusDetail": "Operationally active; broke the sound barrier (Mach 1.1) with its Mk-II Aurora spaceplane in Nov 2024; 207+ thrusters deployed in space and 60+ spaceplane flights completed.", "verifyVerdict": "confirmed", "fundingNotable": "Raised a US$20M Series A in Dec 2022 from Icehouse Ventures, GD1 and Movac at a ~$170M valuation; $42M+ total funding. Icehouse first backed the team in a ~$3.5M seed round in 2018.", "icehouseBacked": "confirmed"}	community:icehouse-ventures	\N	2026-05-31 08:57:29.572	2026-06-01 01:42:36.222	Startup
org:iv-openstar-technologies	organization	OpenStar Technologies	NZ fusion startup pursuing power-positive electricity via levitated dipole reactors	Wellington (Lower Hutt), New Zealand	https://www.openstar.tech/	{"fusion energy",cleantech,"deep tech",superconductors,"levitated dipole","Icehouse Portfolio"}	\N	{"bio": "OpenStar Technologies is a Wellington (Lower Hutt), New Zealand fusion energy startup developing nuclear fusion reactors based on the levitated dipole concept, using high-temperature superconducting magnets to confine plasma. Founded in 2021 by Dr Ratu Mataira out of Victoria University of Wellington's Robinson Research Institute, it aims to deliver power-positive devices to the grid by the early 2030s. In October 2024 it created and confined plasma in its Marsden Class device, holding plasma at roughly 300,000C for 20 seconds. The Maori-led company is backed by Icehouse Ventures among other investors and is building a facility for its next device, Tahi.", "notes": "Icehouse Ventures backing confirmed by Icehouse's own portfolio page and blog (\\"one of Icehouse Venture's latest investments\\") and corroborated by NBR/Outset listing it among seed participants. Seed-round headline figure is NZ$10M / US$6.2M led by Outset Ventures (Aug 2023); cumulative ~NZ$20M by 2024 per Wikipedia. The NZ$35M (Feb 2026) is a government LOAN via the Regional Infrastructure Fund, not equity. Ratu Mataira's LinkedIn verified; Thomas Berry's LinkedIn not located. Do not confuse with unrelated foreign entities sharing the \\"Openstar\\" name.", "stage": "Seed", "status": "active", "founded": "2021", "sources": ["https://www.openstar.tech/", "https://www.openstar.tech/team", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenStar", "https://www.icehouseventures.co.nz/companies/openstar-technologies", "https://resources.icehouseventures.co.nz/blog/no-confusion-about-fusion", "https://www.nbr.co.nz/tech/kiwi-fusion-scientists-get-10m-to-solve-global-energy-question/", "https://outset.ventures/kiwi-fusion-scientists-get-10m-to-solve-global-energy-question/", "https://fusionxinvest.com/news/2195/openstar-technologies-raises-nz10-million-in-seed-round/", "https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/585922/wellington-company-secures-funding-for-clean-fusion-power-facility", "https://businessdesk.co.nz/article/policy/openstar-says-35m-government-loan-will-help-it-stay-in-nz", "https://www.growregions.govt.nz/about-us/news/regional-infrastructure-fund-backs-fusion-energy-facility", "https://www.linkedin.com/in/ratu-mataira-24648ab2/"], "website": "https://www.openstar.tech/", "industry": "Fusion energy / Cleantech", "confidence": "high", "verifyNote": "Both founders confirmed via the official team page (openstar.tech/team): Ratu Mataira (Founder & CEO) and Thomas Berry (Co-founder & Chief Product Officer) — Berry is named only on the company site, not Wikipedia/Icehouse, but is independently sourced so retained. Icehouse backing confirmed (Icehouse portfolio page + blog). Status confirmed active. Corrected the government-fund name from \\"Regional Development Fund\\" to \\"Regional Infrastructure Fund\\" (RNZ/BusinessDesk/NBR/growregions.govt.nz) and clarified it is a loan; added Radar and Aspire to the seed investor list and a Feb 2026 half-tonne magnet levitation milestone. Description tightened for accuracy (early 2030s grid goal per Icehouse).", "statusDetail": "Active and scaling. Achieved first plasma in its Marsden Class device on 31 Oct 2024 (300,000C for 20 seconds); in Feb 2026 levitated a half-tonne superconducting magnet and was announced for a NZ$35M loan from the NZ Government's Regional Infrastructure Fund to build a facility for its next device, Tahi. Reported ~55 staff as of mid-2025.", "verifyVerdict": "corrected", "fundingNotable": "NZ$10M (~US$6.2M) seed round (announced Aug 2023) led by Outset Ventures, with Icehouse Ventures, Blackbird, Radar, Ngai Tahu Holdings, K1W1 and Aspire; ~NZ$20M reported raised by 2024. Separately, a NZ$35M NZ Government loan via the Regional Infrastructure Fund announced Feb 2026 (debt, not equity).", "icehouseBacked": "confirmed"}	community:icehouse-ventures	\N	2026-05-31 08:57:29.572	2026-06-01 01:42:36.222	Startup
