org:iv-basis	organization	Basis	NZ hardware startup building a digitally configurable smart electrical panel to replace the home switchboard.	Auckland, New Zealand	https://www.wearebasis.com	{"energy tech","smart home",hardware,electrification,cleantech,"Icehouse Portfolio"}	\N	{"bio": "Basis is an Auckland-founded New Zealand hardware company building what it calls the world's first digitally configurable smart electrical panel, a software-defined replacement for the traditional home switchboard. The panel lets homeowners monitor and control electricity use in real time, improve safety, and more easily adopt EVs, solar and batteries, with claimed savings of around $1,000+ per year. Founded in 2020 by Danny Purcell and Julyan Collett, it was reported as the first product to achieve IEC 61009 certification for digitally configurable safety. By 2024-2025 it had raised a reported NZ$59m total, secured thousands of contracted pre-orders, and grown to over 100 staff across Auckland and Christchurch.", "notes": "This is the New Zealand energy-tech / smart electrical panel company Basis (wearebasis.com; entity \\"Basis NZ Ltd\\"), NOT the US fintech \\"Basis\\" or any other same-named firm. Icehouse Ventures backing is confirmed: IV maintains a dedicated portfolio page for Basis and is described as its first (seed, 2020) investor with a ~19% stake. Funding figures vary by source/date: NZ Herald (mid-2024) cited ~$38m total at an ~$85m valuation with a ~$26m Series A led by GD1; the Icehouse page and later 2024-2025 coverage cite ~$59m total. HQ is Auckland with R&D in Christchurch.", "stage": "Series A", "status": "active", "founded": "2020", "sources": ["https://www.icehouseventures.co.nz/companies/basis", "https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/companies/basis-the-most-successful-kiwi-startup-youve-never-heard-of/7VROKOGL4VHTJJ4XV2GS2A7U4A/", "https://businessdesk.co.nz/article/technology/auckland-startup-basis-launches-world-first-digital-smart-panel", "https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/kiwi-start-up-basis-launches-smart-panel-to-cut-energy-bills-by-1200/5ZEIM7MZKZCL7OPFS4XIQV5AVM/", "https://www.callaghaninnovation.govt.nz/stories/basis-nz-bringing-electrical-smart-panels-home/", "https://www.nzdownstream.co.nz/people/alex-cappy", "https://nz.linkedin.com/in/danny-purcell-98738320", "https://nz.linkedin.com/in/julyan-collett-17b3442a", "https://www.wearebasis.com"], "website": "https://www.wearebasis.com", "industry": "Hardware / Energy technology (smart electrical panels)", "confidence": "high", "verifyNote": "Corrected Danny Purcell's role from \\"Co-founder & CEO\\" to \\"Co-founder\\": multiple authoritative sources (NZ Herald, NZ Downstream) confirm Alex Cappy is Basis's CEO (COO in 2023, CEO in 2024), so the draft's CEO attribution to Purcell is outdated. Both founders (Purcell, Collett), founding year (2020), Icehouse Ventures seed/first-investor backing (~19% stake), active status, ~$59m total / ~$26m Series A (GD1-led), and Auckland+Christchurch footprint all independently confirmed. Loosened the oneLiner/description's absolute \\"world's first\\" framing to attributed claims. Purcell's exact prior employer could not be pinned down (sources cite both NZ Growth Capital Partners and ACC's investment arm). LinkedIn URLs surfaced as top organic results matching each founder but could not be directly fetched (LinkedIn returns HTTP 999).", "statusDetail": "Active and scaling; smart panel moved out of stealth (July 2024) into commercial launch in New Zealand (soft launch July 2024 / rollout 2025) with 7,500+ contracted orders worth ~$25m, plus planned Australia (late 2025) and UK/Europe/Asia (2026) expansion.", "verifyVerdict": "corrected", "fundingNotable": "Reported total of ~NZ$59m, including a ~$26m Series A in July 2024 led by Global From Day One (GD1, which put in ~$10m). Investors include Icehouse Ventures (first/seed investor in 2020, reported ~19% stake), Global From Day One (~14%), ACC's investment arm (~6%), Movac (~4%), and angels including Sir Stephen Tindall and Phil McCaw. (NZ Herald's mid-2024 piece cited ~$38m total at an ~$85m valuation; later 2024-2025 coverage cites ~$59m total.)", "icehouseBacked": "confirmed"}	community:icehouse-ventures	\N	2026-05-31 08:57:29.572	2026-06-01 01:42:36.222	Startup
org:iv-nuro	organization	Nuro, Inc.	Autonomous-driving company co-founded by Kiwi Dave Ferguson; backed by NZ's Icehouse Ventures.	Mountain View, USA	https://www.nuro.ai	{"autonomous vehicles",robotics,self-driving,delivery,"artificial intelligence","Icehouse Portfolio"}	\N	{"bio": "Nuro is a US-based autonomous-driving technology company founded in 2016 by Jiajun Zhu and New Zealander Dave Ferguson, both former principal engineers on Google's self-driving car project (now Waymo). It originally built custom electric autonomous delivery vehicles, then pivoted in 2024 to licensing its \\"Nuro Driver\\" Level 4 autonomy software and hardware to automakers and mobility providers. It is developing a robotaxi program with Uber using Lucid vehicles. New Zealand's Icehouse Ventures invested in Nuro's 2025 Series E round.", "notes": "DISAMBIGUATION: The only \\"Nuro\\" backed by Icehouse Ventures is the US autonomous-vehicle company (Mountain View, CA), which is \\"Kiwi\\" via NZ-born co-founder Dave Ferguson. No separate NZ brain/health company named \\"Nuro\\" exists in Icehouse's portfolio; do not conflate with Canadian neurotech \\"NURO Corp.\\" (nuro.world) or NZ \\"Neuro\\"-named clinics. Jiajun Zhu's LinkedIn URL was not reliably confirmed, so left null.", "stage": "Series E (Late-stage / Growth)", "status": "active", "founded": "2016", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuro", "https://www.nuro.ai/company", "https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20251022547092/en/Nuro-Appoints-Co-Founder-Dave-Ferguson-as-Co-CEO-to-Accelerate-Commercial-Growth", "https://techcrunch.com/2025/08/21/nvidia-is-latest-investor-to-back-av-startup-nuro-in-203m-funding-round/", "https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/robotaxis-self-driving-start-up-nuro-founded-by-kiwi-dave-ferguson-raises-us203m-at-a-us6b-valuation-in-funding-round-backed-by-uber-nvidia/FDLS4XENRFANLCOPW3GQ2YJKTQ/", "https://keanewzealand.com/world-class-winner-dave-ferguson/", "https://www.linkedin.com/in/dave-ferguson-565b974", "https://www.icehouseventures.co.nz/portfolio"], "website": "https://www.nuro.ai", "industry": "Autonomous vehicles / robotics", "confidence": "high", "verifyNote": "Founders (Jiajun Zhu, Dave Ferguson), the Oct 22 2025 dual Co-CEO appointment, Ferguson's Otago/CMU/Waymo/Mars/Kea 2022 background, the 2024 licensing pivot, the ~US$6b valuation, and Icehouse Ventures' US$5m participation all independently confirmed. Corrected the funding-round YEAR: the US$203m Series E was raised/closed in 2025 (US$106m in April 2025, closed August 2025), not 2024 — the draft conflated the 2024 strategy pivot with the round. Refined total raised to ~US$2.3b (draft said \\"over US$2b\\") and added the US$8.6b (2021 Series D) to US$6b valuation drop. Stage clarified as Series E. Jiajun Zhu's LinkedIn left null (unconfirmed).", "statusDetail": "Active and independent; in Oct 2025 co-founder Dave Ferguson (formerly President) was named Co-CEO alongside co-founder and Co-CEO Jiajun Zhu. Pivoted to licensing its autonomy software in 2024.", "verifyVerdict": "corrected", "fundingNotable": "Closed a US$203m Series E in August 2025 (US$106m initial in April 2025 led by T. Rowe Price and Fidelity, plus US$97m), at a ~US$6b valuation (down from US$8.6b at its 2021 Series D). New investors included Nvidia, Uber, Kindred Ventures, Pledge Ventures and NZ's Icehouse Ventures, which contributed US$5m. Has raised ~US$2.3b in total.", "icehouseBacked": "confirmed"}	community:icehouse-ventures	\N	2026-05-31 08:57:29.572	2026-06-01 01:42:36.222	Startup
org:iv-powerbyproxi	organization	PowerbyProxi	NZ wireless power pioneer, spun out of University of Auckland; acquired by Apple in 2017.	Auckland, New Zealand	https://nz.linkedin.com/company/powerbyproxi	{"wireless power","wireless charging",hardware,"deep tech",semiconductors,"Icehouse Portfolio",Acquired}	\N	{"bio": "PowerbyProxi was an Auckland, New Zealand company that designed and licensed wireless (inductive/resonant) power and charging technology for consumer and industrial applications. Founded in 2007 as a University of Auckland Engineering School spin-out built on roughly three decades of wireless power research, it grew into one of the world's largest wireless power firms by revenue, holding 500+ patents and licensing to major customers. It went through The Icehouse incubation programme and raised venture funding led by Movac, with later investment from Samsung Ventures and TE Connectivity. Apple acquired the company in October 2017.", "notes": "Three co-founders confirmed: Mishriki and Bhargava are consistently named (they entered the 2003 Spark $40k Challenge together); Greg Cross is named as the third co-founder in The Icehouse case study, though Movac's portfolio page lists only Mishriki and Cross. Founding year 2007 per both The Icehouse and other sources; the underlying tech traces to ~three decades of University of Auckland (Uniservices) research. Acquisition reported by most outlets as October 2017 (the University of Auckland alumni page says 2018, an outlier). Acquisition price undisclosed (reported >US$100m); NZ$270m is an Apple internal asset-transfer valuation per NZ Herald, not a stated sale price. Founder LinkedIn URLs for Mishriki and Cross not pinned to a verified profile, so left null. Not to be confused with any same-named foreign entity.", "stage": "Series D", "status": "acquired", "founded": "2007", "sources": ["https://www.theicehouse.co.nz/blog/casestudy/powerbyproxi-powering-up-the-business", "https://www.movac.co.nz/portfolio/power-by-proxi/", "https://www.cnbc.com/2017/10/25/apple-acquires-new-zealand-wireless-charging-company-powerbyproxi.html", "https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/apple-buys-kiwi-tech-company-powerbyproxi/VOQMOKIPDPOLCFVUOQXJAREWWM/", "https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/apples-asset-shuffling-puts-270m-value-on-powerbyproxi/TWC35R2PWECEV5YIBZPX2HWXF4/", "https://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/BU1309/S01103/michael-pachos-joins-powerbyproxis-board.htm", "https://www.prnewswire.co.uk/news-releases/powerbyproxi-announces-4-million-in-strategic-funding-from-samsung-ventures-225782921.html", "https://www.keanewzealand.com/kea_winners/fady-mishriki/", "https://www.auckland.ac.nz/en/alumni/our-alumni/40-under-40/a-z-list-of-profiles/kunal-bhargava.html", "https://nz.linkedin.com/company/powerbyproxi"], "website": "https://nz.linkedin.com/company/powerbyproxi", "industry": "Wireless power / charging technology", "confidence": "high", "verifyNote": "Independent fresh sources (The Icehouse case study, Movac portfolio, CNBC, NZ Herald, Scoop/PR Newswire, Kea NZ, University of Auckland) confirm the draft's core claims: 2007 founding, University of Auckland spin-out, three co-founders (Mishriki/CEO, Bhargava/engineering, Cross), The Icehouse 2007 incubation, Movac lead through Series D, Samsung Ventures (~US$4m, Sept 2013, Michael Pachos board seat) and TE Connectivity (~US$5m) investments, and Apple's October 2017 acquisition. Minor refinement: TE Connectivity's investment was part of the ~US$9m Series C (it was not merely 'an investor'), now reflected in fundingNotable. icehouseBacked left 'likely' — Icehouse incubation/network involvement is confirmed, but no source confirms a direct Icehouse Ventures equity investment (documented equity investors were Movac, Samsung Ventures, TE Connectivity). Confidence raised to high.", "statusDetail": "Acquired by Apple (October 2017); team continued at Apple's Auckland Technology Centre. Price was undisclosed but reported as north of US$100m; NZ Herald reported Apple's internal asset transfers implied a ~NZ$270m valuation (not a stated sale price).", "verifyVerdict": "confirmed", "fundingNotable": "VC rounds led by Movac (initial round through to Series D). Series C brought in TE Connectivity (~US$5m) and Samsung Ventures (~US$4m, announced Sept 2013), with Samsung's Michael Pachos joining the board alongside a wireless charging partnership. Acquired by Apple in October 2017.", "icehouseBacked": "likely"}	community:icehouse-ventures	\N	2026-05-31 08:57:29.572	2026-06-01 01:42:36.222	Startup
org:iv-lawvu	organization	LawVu	Connected legal workspace for in-house legal teams — matter, contract and spend management	Tauranga, New Zealand	https://lawvu.com	{LegalTech,SaaS,"Enterprise Software","Contract Management","Legal AI","Icehouse Portfolio"}	\N	{"bio": "LawVu is a New Zealand-founded legal technology company offering a cloud-based \\"legal workspace\\" that unifies matter, contract, and spend management for corporate in-house legal teams. Founded in 2015 in Tauranga by Sam Kidd and Tim Boyne, it has expanded internationally with offices in New Zealand, Australia, the US and Ireland and customers including Expedia, PwC, Discord, Etsy and KPMG. In December 2025 it acquired Belgian contract-automation firm ClauseBase (rebranded LawVu Draft) and launched its LawVu Lens AI contract-analysis engine, reaching a reported NZ$400m valuation. It is venture-backed, with investors including Insight Partners, Movac, AirTree Ventures and Icehouse Ventures.", "notes": "This is the NZ (Tauranga) legaltech company, distinct from any same-named foreign entities. Icehouse backing confirmed: LawVu appears on Icehouse Ventures' job board and its own funding release names Icehouse Ventures (with a quote from CEO Robbie Paul) as an early investor. Founders consistently reported as Sam Kidd and Tim Boyne; some aggregators (e.g. Tracxn) list \\"Shaun Plant\\" as a co-founder, but other sources describe him as recruited by Kidd (now Chief Legal Officer / community evangelist), so he is omitted as a founder. Tim Boyne departed c.2022 and is now CEO of SmartSpace.ai. Funding figures mix NZD/USD across rounds; cumulative total is approximate.", "stage": "Later-stage / growth (reported NZ$400m valuation as of December 2025)", "status": "active", "founded": "2015", "sources": ["https://jobs.icehouseventures.co.nz/companies/lawvu", "https://lawvu.com/news/capital-injection-cements-international-growth-for-legal-tech-company-lawvu/", "https://idealog.co.nz/venture/2023/10/a-conversation-with-one-of-new-zealands-most-successful-start-ups-lawvu", "https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/business/498428/cloud-based-legal-software-firm-lawvu-gets-15m-investment-from-movac", "https://techcrunch.com/2021/08/09/lawvu-a-cloud-based-platform-for-in-house-legal-teams-raises-17m-nzd-from-insight-partners/", "https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/lawvu-hits-400m-valuation-buys-european-firm-clausebase-to-boost-its-ai-push/premium/CVLQ3ZT3UNB7HMIIYUVZQE2LYQ/", "https://www.lawfuel.com/lawvu-hits-400m-valuation-and-goes-full-speed-on-legal-ai/", "https://www.artificiallawyer.com/2025/12/17/lawvu-buys-clausebase-for-contract-ai-expansion/", "https://lawvu.com/articles/announcing-the-acquisition-of-clausebase-and-launch-of-lawvu-lens/", "https://nz.linkedin.com/in/tim-boyne"], "website": "https://lawvu.com", "industry": "Legal Technology (SaaS)", "confidence": "high", "verifyNote": "Founders (Sam Kidd, Tim Boyne), 2015 Tauranga founding, the legal-workspace product, the Dec 2025 ClauseBase acquisition/LawVu Draft rebrand + LawVu Lens launch, ~NZ$400m valuation, ~160 staff (~100 NZ), and Icehouse Ventures backing all independently confirmed. Corrected: the draft conflated the NZ$15m Movac round (actually September 2023, NOT late 2025) with the December 2025 $400m/ClauseBase event — fundingNotable rewritten. The draft's specific \\"US$6m raised at NZ$400m valuation\\" and \\"~NZ$28m ClauseBase price\\" figures could not be confirmed in freely accessible sources (NZ Herald premium paywalled), so they were softened to undisclosed. Tim Boyne's departure refined to c.2022 (now CEO of SmartSpace.ai); his prior role corrected to ~10 years in IT at law firm Sharp Tudhope. \\"Shaun Plant\\" correctly remains excluded as a founder.", "statusDetail": "Active and operating; ~160 staff globally (~100 in NZ), HQ in Tauranga. Acquired ClauseBase (Belgium) in December 2025, rebranded as LawVu Draft. LawVu has itself been an acquirer, not acquired.", "verifyVerdict": "corrected", "fundingNotable": "Total funding reported around NZ$55m. Rounds include an early NZ$3.65m round (led by Kent Gardner, with Icehouse Ventures participating, c.2020), NZ$17m from Insight Partners (2021), a Series A reportedly extended to ~NZ$37m (2022), and NZ$15m led by Movac in September 2023. In December 2025 LawVu reached a reported NZ$400m valuation alongside its ClauseBase acquisition and LawVu Lens launch; specific new-capital amounts and the ClauseBase purchase price tied to that event were not disclosed in freely accessible sources.", "icehouseBacked": "confirmed"}	community:icehouse-ventures	\N	2026-05-31 08:57:29.572	2026-06-01 01:42:36.222	Startup
org:iv-partly	organization	Partly	Building the data infrastructure that connects the world's auto parts supply chain.	Christchurch, New Zealand	https://www.partly.com	{"auto parts","B2B SaaS","data infrastructure",automotive,AI,"Icehouse Portfolio"}	\N	{"bio": "Partly is a New Zealand software company digitising the global automotive parts industry, building infrastructure that connects the parts supply chain so manufacturers, retailers and online marketplaces can manage, source and sell parts and match buyers to the correct vehicle-specific parts. Its data assets span tens of millions of parts from thousands of suppliers, and it now positions AI products such as its \\"Interpreter\\" parts foundation model and \\"PerfectPart\\" supply-chain model at the core. Founded in 2020 and headquartered in Christchurch, it has expanded across New Zealand, Australia, Europe and the US.", "notes": "This is the Christchurch, New Zealand auto-parts data company at partly.com (Partly Group Limited), not a same-named foreign company. Icehouse Ventures backing is confirmed in two rounds: a named seed co-investor (Oct 2020, per Scoop/NZ Entrepreneur) and a Series A participant (Dec 2022, per Startup Daily/RNZ); TechCrunch's Series A write-up did not enumerate Icehouse but NZ media did. Tony Austin (now Chief Strategy Officer) was an early angel investor and is loosely called a co-founder in one Icehouse \\"Startup Blueprint\\" article, but is more consistently reported as an exec/investor rather than an original founder, so he is excluded from the founders array. Latest publicly confirmed raise is the Dec 2022 Series A; no later round (e.g., Series B) was found in authoritative sources as of 2026-05-31. The \\"$50M valuation\\" in one NZ Herald headline refers to the earlier pre-Series A round, not the Series A.", "stage": "Series A", "status": "active", "founded": "2020", "sources": ["https://www.partly.com/about", "https://nzentrepreneur.co.nz/partly-raises-1-7-million-seed-funding-from-all-star-consortium/", "https://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/BU2010/S00131/partly-raises-17-million-in-seed-funding-from-all-star-consortium-of-investors.htm", "https://www.startupdaily.net/topic/funding/kiwi-car-parts-startup-partly-banks-record-nz37-million-series/", "https://techcrunch.com/2022/12/12/ex-rocket-lab-engineer-raises-21m-for-partly-to-make-buying-car-parts-easier/", "https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/business/480668/car-parts-company-partly-raises-37m-in-capital", "https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/partly-founded-an-ex-rocket-lab-engineer-closes-largest-series-a-raise-in-nz-history/KXWKLU3NLBFDHMW436L3FTMXDE/", "https://resources.icehouseventures.co.nz/blog/startup-blueprint-levifawcett", "https://jobs.icehouseventures.co.nz/companies/partly-2"], "website": "https://www.partly.com", "industry": "Automotive parts software / B2B SaaS", "confidence": "high", "verifyNote": "Independently confirmed via fresh searches. All four founders (Fawcett CEO, Taylor COO, Song Head of Marketing, Jia Head of Engineering) verified by NZ Entrepreneur, Startup Daily and the Icehouse blueprint. Founding (2020), Christchurch HQ, the US$1.7M seed (Blackbird-led, Icehouse + Hillfarrance co-investors), the NZ$3.7M pre-Series A, and the record NZ$37M/~US$21M Series A (Octopus-led, ~NZ$180M valuation) all confirmed. Icehouse Ventures backing confirmed in both seed and Series A. Minor edits: shortened the oneLiner, dropped the unverifiable \\"Forbes 30 Under 30\\" / \\"2024 NZ Leadership Awards overall winner\\" claims from bios (replaced Fawcett's with the verifiable EY Entrepreneur of the Year finalist recognition), and noted the company's current AI-product positioning (Interpreter / PerfectPart) on its own site. No funding round later than the Dec 2022 Series A could be confirmed.", "statusDetail": "Active and independent. Latest confirmed round: NZ$37M Series A (December 2022, ~US$21M, reported at a ~NZ$180M valuation), described as New Zealand's largest Series A at the time.", "verifyVerdict": "confirmed", "fundingNotable": "Seed US$1.7M (Oct 2020, led by Blackbird Ventures with Hillfarrance and Icehouse Ventures as co-investors, plus angels including Peter Beck); NZ$3.7M pre-Series A (Oct 2021); NZ$37M Series A (~US$21M, Dec 2022) led by Octopus Ventures with Square Peg, Blackbird, Shasta Ventures, Ten13, Hillfarrance, I2BF, Icehouse Ventures and Square co-founder Randy Reddig. Rocket Lab founder Peter Beck is an early backer/adviser.", "icehouseBacked": "confirmed"}	community:icehouse-ventures	\N	2026-05-31 08:57:29.572	2026-06-01 01:42:36.222	Startup
org:iv-number-8-bio	organization	Number 8 Bio	Synthetic-biology agritech making BetterFeed additives that cut cattle methane emissions	Sydney, Australia	https://www.number8.bio/	{agritech,"synthetic biology","methane reduction","livestock feed","climate tech","Icehouse Portfolio"}	\N	{"bio": "Number 8 Bio is a synthetic-biology agritech company developing livestock feed additives (branded BetterFeed) that reduce enteric methane emissions from cattle while improving farm productivity. Founded in 2022 by Dr Tom Williams (CEO) and Dr Alex Carpenter (CSO) and spun out of Macquarie University's Australian Genome Foundry, it is headquartered in Sydney, Australia. In 2025 it raised an AU$11 million Series A led by New Zealand's Icehouse Ventures, following a ~AU$7m seed round in September 2024 led by Main Sequence. Funds target large-scale animal trials and regulatory approvals across New Zealand, Europe and Australia, with a feed additive and slow-release capsule planned for 2026.", "notes": "Disambiguation: although researched as an \\"NZ company,\\" Number 8 Bio is HQ'd in Sydney, Australia. The NZ links are (1) co-founder/CEO Tom Williams, who is from the Waikato and trained at University of Waikato, and (2) lead investor Icehouse Ventures, a New Zealand VC. Icehouse backing is firmly confirmed: it led the 2025 AU$11m Series A per Icehouse's own portfolio page, NBR and Startup Daily. The company spun out of Macquarie University's Australian Genome Foundry / ARC Centre of Excellence in Synthetic Biology. The \\"Number 8\\" name plausibly alludes to NZ's \\"number 8 wire\\" ingenuity ethos, but no primary source explicitly states this — treat as inference. Series A is AU$11m (~USD 7.1-7.3m); seed-round size is reported inconsistently (~A$7m per Startup Daily vs ~$4.5m in some aggregator summaries). Founder bios are summarized factually from the SynBio Australasia interview; the company team page lists names/roles but no individual bio text.", "stage": "Series A", "status": "active", "founded": "2022", "sources": ["https://www.icehouseventures.co.nz/companies/number-8-bio", "https://www.number8.bio/our-team", "https://www.synbioaustralasia.org/news/number-8-bio-interview-tom-williams-and-alex-carpenter", "https://www.startupdaily.net/topic/funding/livestock-methane-reduction-startup-swallows-11-million-series-a/", "https://www.startupdaily.net/topic/funding/agtech-startup-number-8-bio-swallows-7-million-seed-round-to-cut-livestock-methane/", "https://www.nbr.co.nz/tech/methane-reducing-feed-startup-number-8-bio-raises-a11m/", "https://www.mq.edu.au/partner/access-business-opportunities/innovation-entrepreneurship-and-it/incubator/news/news-items/feeding-the-world-saving-the-planet", "https://agfundernews.com/number-8-bio-ceo-synthetic-chemistry-not-seaweed-or-biomanufacturing-is-key-to-cost-effective-livestock-methane-reduction"], "website": "https://www.number8.bio/", "industry": "Agritech / Synthetic biology", "confidence": "high", "verifyNote": "Independently confirmed via the company team page, Icehouse Ventures' portfolio page, Startup Daily, NBR and the SynBio Australasia interview: both founders (Dr Tom Williams - CEO; Dr Alex Carpenter - CSO) are real with correct names/roles; status is active; AU$11m Series A led by Icehouse Ventures is confirmed (Icehouse backing solid). Minor edits to the draft: clarified Series A year as \\"2025\\" (reported Nov/Dec 2025) rather than asserting a precise month; added Australia to the regulatory-target geographies and Breakthrough Victoria/The March Group to seed backers; added the Macquarie Australian Genome Foundry spin-out origin. Flagged that seed-round size is reported inconsistently and that the \\"number 8 wire\\" name origin is an unconfirmed inference.", "statusDetail": "Active; raised AU$11m Series A (2025) led by Icehouse Ventures, targeting commercial release of a feed additive and slow-release cattle capsule in 2026", "verifyVerdict": "confirmed", "fundingNotable": "AU$11m Series A (2025) led by Icehouse Ventures, with Main Sequence and Japanese climate VC One Innovators; prior ~AU$7m seed (Sept 2024) led by Main Sequence, with Breakthrough Victoria and The March Group", "icehouseBacked": "confirmed"}	community:icehouse-ventures	\N	2026-05-31 08:57:29.572	2026-06-01 01:42:36.222	Startup
org:iv-foxglove	organization	Foxglove	Data and observability platform for Physical AI and robotics development	San Francisco, USA	https://foxglove.dev	{robotics,"Physical AI","developer tools","data observability",SaaS,"Icehouse Portfolio"}	\N	{"bio": "Foxglove builds a data and observability platform for robotics and \\"Physical AI,\\" offering open-source and commercial tools that let teams capture, visualize, analyze, and manage multimodal sensor data (3D, video, audio, GNSS) to develop and deploy robots faster. It was founded in 2021 by New Zealander Adrian Macneil (CEO) and Roman Shtylman (CTO), who previously worked together at autonomous-vehicle company Cruise and at Coinbase. Its tools are used by tens of thousands of developers at companies including NVIDIA, Amazon, Anduril, and Wayve. The company is headquartered in San Francisco and raised a US$40M Series B in late 2025.", "notes": "NZ-founded but US-headquartered (San Francisco) — not a domestically-based NZ company, despite the Icehouse Ventures connection and Kiwi founder Adrian Macneil. At its 2022 Series A the company was described as fully remote, but San Francisco is its stated HQ (per Icehouse and Crunchbase). Do not confuse with: the UK legal charity \\"Foxglove\\" (foxglove.org.uk), or \\"Foxglove Florist\\" (foxgloveflorist.co.nz), which are unrelated same-named entities. Founders' city-level locations are inferred from the company's San Francisco HQ; exact residences not individually confirmed.", "stage": "Series B", "status": "active", "founded": "2021", "sources": ["https://www.icehouseventures.co.nz/companies/foxglove", "https://foxglove.dev/about", "https://foxglove.dev/blog/foxglove-series-b", "https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/ai-robotics-firm-foxglove-headed-by-kiwi-adrian-macneil-raises-us40m/VU25Y7ELFZH5ZN2JHJL7MWP5PQ/", "https://techcrunch.com/2022/10/11/foxglove-raises-16m-to-build-dev-infrastructure-for-robots/", "https://www.linkedin.com/in/adrianmacneil", "https://www.linkedin.com/in/roman-shtylman-5b8462165/"], "website": "https://foxglove.dev", "industry": "Robotics developer tools / data infrastructure software", "confidence": "high", "verifyNote": "Fresh searches independently confirmed all major claims. Founders Adrian Macneil (CEO) and Roman Shtylman (CTO), 2021 founding, and SF HQ are confirmed via foxglove.dev/about, the Icehouse portfolio page, and TechCrunch. Icehouse Ventures backing is CONFIRMED — explicitly named in Foxglove's own Series B blog post and listed on Icehouse's portfolio page. The US$40M Series B led by Bessemer (late 2025) and the ~US$15M Series A led by Eclipse (2022) are both confirmed; the draft's Series B lead is correct (Bessemer, not Eclipse). The \\"data and observability platform for Physical AI\\" description matches Foxglove's and Icehouse's own wording. Only minor edits: clarified that the Series A lead is \\"Eclipse Ventures\\" and added the SF-HQ-vs-remote nuance to notes; Roman Shtylman's prior background lightly broadened (Polychain Labs also appears). Confidence remains high.", "statusDetail": "Active and independent; raised a US$40M Series B in late 2025 (led by Bessemer Venture Partners), having previously raised a ~US$15M Series A in 2022 (led by Eclipse Ventures).", "verifyVerdict": "confirmed", "fundingNotable": "US$40M Series B (late 2025) led by Bessemer Venture Partners (Alexandra Sukin, Jeremy Levine), with participation from existing investors Eclipse and Amplify Partners and new investors including NZ's Icehouse Ventures; earlier ~US$15M Series A (2022) led by Eclipse Ventures.", "icehouseBacked": "confirmed"}	community:icehouse-ventures	\N	2026-05-31 08:57:29.572	2026-06-01 01:42:36.222	Startup
org:iv-genoapay	organization	Genoapay	Auckland BNPL fintech; interest-free 10-week instalments. Acquired by Latitude (2018), closed Apr 2023.	Auckland, New Zealand	https://genoapay.com	{fintech,BNPL,payments,"consumer finance","Icehouse Portfolio",Acquired}	\N	{"bio": "Genoapay was an Auckland, New Zealand buy-now-pay-later (BNPL) fintech that launched in market in August 2016, letting shoppers split purchases of up to NZ$1,000 into 10 weekly interest-free payments, with a focus on service sectors such as automotive, dental and veterinary. It was founded by CEO Shaun Quincey (co-founded with Damon O'Neill) and was acquired by ASX-listed Latitude Financial Services, announced on 4 December 2018. Under Latitude it sat alongside the Australian LatitudePay BNPL brand. Latitude announced on 24 February 2023 that it would exit BNPL in New Zealand and Australia, citing regulatory uncertainty and the service being only about 0.3% of group receivables, with the service stopping on 11 April 2023.", "notes": "Disambiguation: this is the New Zealand (Auckland) BNPL fintech Genoapay, distinct from any same-named foreign entities. Icehouse Ventures backing is confirmed: Genoapay went through The Icehouse's Flux Accelerator and is listed as a Tuhua Ventures (Icehouse-managed fund) portfolio company. Shaun Quincey is the founder/CEO across all primary sources (the Latitude press release names only him). Damon O'Neill is independently named as a Genoapay co-founder by NBR and Startup Daily, though his exact founding title there is not specified in primary sources.", "stage": "Acquired", "status": "acquired", "founded": "2016", "sources": ["https://www.latitudefinancial.com.au/about-us/media-releases/latitude-financial-services-acquires-auckland-based-fintech-genoapay.html", "https://www.latitudefinancial.com.au/about-us/media-releases/latitude-closes-its-bnpl-service-in-australia-and-new-zealand.html", "https://www.gemfinance.co.nz/genoapay/closure/", "https://www.callaghaninnovation.govt.nz/news-and-events/top-startup-genoapay-attracts-record-accelerator-investment", "https://www.nbr.co.nz/business/genoapay-founders-launch-new-insurance-payment-platform-simfuni/", "https://www.startupdaily.net/topic/funding/kiwi-insurtech-simfuni-banks-nz2-8-million-in-series-a/", "https://www.cbinsights.com/investor/tuhua-ventures", "https://www.b2bspeakers.co.nz/speakers/shaun-quincey", "https://nz.linkedin.com/in/shaun-quincey-53548132", "https://nz.linkedin.com/in/damon-o-neill-8157171b"], "website": "https://genoapay.com", "industry": "Fintech / Buy Now Pay Later (BNPL)", "confidence": "high", "verifyNote": "Core facts confirmed via fresh searches. Quincey (founder/CEO) and O'Neill (co-founder) both independently sourced (Latitude release names only Quincey; NBR and Startup Daily name both as Genoapay co-founders). Acquisition by Latitude confirmed (announced 4 Dec 2018). Refined the closure timeline: Latitude announced the BNPL exit on 24 Feb 2023 and the service closed 11 April 2023 (draft's 'April 2023' was the effective close). Confirmed Aug 2016 launch and NZ$1,000 / 10-week interest-free terms. Icehouse backing confirmed (Flux Accelerator + Tuhua Ventures fund). Removed the misleading 'insurtech-adjacent' tag (insurtech relates to the founders' later venture Simfuni, not Genoapay). The ~NZ$10m early valuation remains secondary-media-only and is flagged as unconfirmed. Could not re-fetch the Callaghan Innovation article (404) or the sharechat valuation page (connection refused), but their substance was corroborated elsewhere.", "statusDetail": "Acquired by Latitude Financial Services (announced Dec 2018); operated alongside LatitudePay. Latitude announced its BNPL exit on 24 Feb 2023; the service closed in NZ and Australia on 11 April 2023.", "verifyVerdict": "confirmed", "fundingNotable": "Raised ~NZ$1m at a Flux Accelerator (delivered by The Icehouse) Demo Day, reported as a record NZ Demo Day pitch; backed through the Icehouse-managed Tuhua Ventu