is retained. Minor edits only: tightened the description (DMC casts from digital files/CAD; the public material emphasises microwave melting rather than 3D-printed ceramic molds), refined the alloy list to those explicitly cited, and softened the Series B/amount wording as unconfirmed. Founder LinkedIn URL could not be directly fetched (page blocks bots) but is consistently attributed across sources.", "statusDetail": "Active and commercializing. Announced first commercial sale of a DMC-3 system to the New Jersey Innovation Institute (US Army DEVCOM Armaments Center / COMET partnership) in Nov 2024. Operates a US global HQ in Austin, TX plus R&D in Petone, Lower Hutt, Wellington, NZ.", "verifyVerdict": "confirmed", "fundingNotable": "US$8M Series A (~NZ$12M) in Nov 2021, led by Blackbird, with Icehouse Ventures, GD1, K1W1, WNT Ventures, Founders Fund and Promus Ventures; earlier 2020 backing from Founders Fund & Promus Ventures. Angel/individual backers reported include Rocket Lab founder Peter Beck, Sir Stephen Tindall, Motional CEO Karl Iagnemma and former Autodesk CEO Carl Bass. Total raised reported ~US$14M as of 2024; a Series B was reported as being prepared, amount and close unconfirmed.", "icehouseBacked": "confirmed"}	community:icehouse-ventures	\N	2026-05-31 08:57:29.572	2026-06-01 01:42:36.222	Startup
org:iv-biolumic	organization	BioLumic	Precise UV light treatments that activate non-GMO seed traits to boost crop yield and resilience	Palmerston North, New Zealand	https://www.biolumic.com	{agritech,"seed technology","UV photobiology","crop science","sustainable agriculture","Icehouse Portfolio"}	\N	{"bio": "BioLumic is a New Zealand- and US-based agritech company that applies precise, short-duration ultraviolet (UV) light treatments to seeds to activate beneficial, non-GMO plant traits that improve crop yield, vigour and resilience. Founded in 2013 by plant UV photobiologist Dr Jason Wargent out of his research at Massey University, it runs its patented \\"xTraits\\" light-signalling platform from Palmerston North, New Zealand, with US operations. In 2025 it launched commercial light-activated corn seed traits (with US deployment targeted for the 2026 planting season), began expanding into South America, and announced a multi-year forage/ryegrass partnership with Fonterra. Field trials with Fonterra are slated to start in 2026, with a commercial pasture product targeted for 2028.", "notes": "This is the Palmerston North / Massey University agritech firm, distinct from any same-named foreign entities. Icehouse Ventures backing is confirmed: Icehouse Ventures is explicitly named as an investor in BioLumic's official June 2022 Series B press release. Founded 2013 by Dr Jason Wargent (sole, consistently documented founder), who serves as Chief Science Officer. CEO is Steve Sibulkin (appointed June 2020, NOT a founder) — correctly excluded from the founders list. COO is Greg Levow. The April 2025 Series B Extension was led by Fonterra's Ki Tua Fund, which connects to the Sept 2025 Fonterra forage partnership. Total cumulative funding figures vary across databases and are not authoritatively confirmed. The founder LinkedIn URL is from search results and was not directly fetched (LinkedIn blocks automated access).", "stage": "Series B", "status": "active", "founded": "20