org:iv-grater-goods	organization	Grater Goods	NZ artisanal plant-based charcuterie, cheese and deli producer (in liquidation, Nov 2025).	Christchurch, New Zealand	https://gratergoods.co.nz	{plant-based,vegan,"food & beverage",deli/charcuterie,FMCG,"Icehouse Portfolio",Closed}	\N	{"bio": "Grater Goods was a Christchurch-based New Zealand company making gourmet plant-based delicatessen products including vegan charcuterie, \\"meats,\\" cheeses, spreads and pates, alongside a contract-manufacturing arm and a vegan bistro. It was founded in 2018 by musician-turned-entrepreneur Clare \\"Flip\\" Grater and her French husband Youssef Iskrane, pitching itself as a European-style plant-based deli first-mover. The business was placed into voluntary liquidation on 7 November 2025 after a Woolworths supply deal lined up in early 2024 was put on hold and ultimately lost, leaving a working-capital squeeze. Flip Grater described the company as \\"simply a victim of the current economy.\\"", "notes": "This is the Christchurch (Sydenham), NZ company, distinct from any same-named foreign entity. The brand also operated a bistro/restaurant under the \\"Grater Good\\" name (opened 2025, closed after ~7 months); \\"Grater Goods\\" is the manufacturing/deli company. Icehouse backing confirmed via NZ Herald and The Press reporting on the liquidation: ArcAngels fund invested NZ$80k in 2021 and held 8.29% at liquidation among 11 shareholders. Co-founder Youssef Iskrane is named by NZ food/business media (NZ Herald, The Press, planetfood) as Flip Grater's French husband, but his exact corporate title and LinkedIn could not be independently confirmed and Wikipedia's Flip Grater article does not mention him.", "stage": "Pre-seed / equity crowdfunding", "status": "closed", "founded": "2018", "sources": ["https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/companies/retail/plant-based-food-firm-grater-goods-goes-into-liquidation-with-debts-owed-to-asb-and-ird/premium/GJIX2JI4MZCWNLUZQLVAQU2H6I/", "https://www.thepress.co.nz/nz-news/360878862/guttinggrater-goods-voluntary-liquidation", "https://www.planetfood.news/post/nz-s-grater-goods-raises-nearly-250-000-in-early-funding-sets-sights-on-expanding-presence-in-anz", "https://www.equitise.com/offer/grater-goods-offer/", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flip_Grater", "https://nz.linkedin.com/company/grater-goods", "https://www.linkedin.com/in/flip-grater-62388520b/"], "website": "https://gratergoods.co.nz", "industry": "Plant-based / vegan food manufacturing", "confidence": "high", "verifyNote": "Independently confirmed all core claims via NZ Herald, The Press, planetfood.news and Wikipedia: founders (Flip Grater founder/CEO + Youssef Iskrane co-founder/husband), status (voluntary liquidation 7 Nov 2025, liquidator Brenton Hunt of Insolvency Matters), Icehouse Ventures/ArcAngels backing (NZ$80k 2021 pre-seed, 8.29% of 11 shareholders), Equitise crowdfund (~NZ$266,400 vs NZ$300k minimum), and the lost Woolworths deal (early 2024, category put on hold). Corrections/enrichments: the draft's statusDetail listed only ASB and IRD debts — broadened to the four secured creditors (ASB, Bidfood, Trents Wholesale, Southern Hospitality, ~NZ$108.5k), ~NZ$60k IRD, ~NZ$10k staff, ~NZ$100k unsecured, ~NZ$208.5k total shortfall. Refined Flip Grater's CEO title and album-specific bio. Youssef Iskrane's precise title/LinkedIn remain unconfirmed (kept, with caveat). Confidence remains high.", "statusDetail": "Placed into voluntary liquidation on 7 November 2025 (liquidator Brenton Hunt, Insolvency Matters). Four registered secured creditors totalling ~NZ$108,500 (incl. ASB Bank general security, Bidfood, Trents Wholesale, Southern Hospitality); ~NZ$60k owed to Inland Revenue (GST/PAYE), ~NZ$10k staff wages/holiday pay, and ~NZ$100k to unsecured creditors; estimated total shortfall ~NZ$208,500.", "verifyVerdict": "confirmed", "fundingNotable": "Icehouse Ventures' ArcAngels fund (women-led startups) invested NZ$80,000 in a 2021 pre-seed round, holding an 8.29% minority stake among 11 shareholders at liquidation. Also ran a 2023 equity crowdfund on Equitise, raising ~NZ$266,400 against a NZ$300,000 minimum (NZ$1.5m max first-round target).", "icehouseBacked": "confirmed"}	community:icehouse-ventures	\N	2026-05-31 08:57:29.572	2026-06-01 01:42:36.222	Startup
org:iv-hot-lime-labs	organization	Hot Lime Labs	Renewable CO2 and heat from waste biomass for commercial greenhouses	Lower Hutt, New Zealand	https://hotlimelabs.com	{cleantech,agritech,"carbon capture",decarbonisation,"greenhouse CO2","Icehouse Portfolio"}	\N	{"bio": "Hot Lime Labs is a New Zealand cleantech/agritech company founded in 2017 that produces clean, renewable CO2 for commercial greenhouses. Its patented limestone-pellet technology captures CO2 from the combustion of waste wood and biomass, then releases concentrated CO2 and heat on demand to boost greenhouse crop yields. The technology was originally developed at Callaghan Innovation, and the greenhouse application is now commercialised through a partner called Aplenty. The company operated a commercial unit at the Gourmet Mokai greenhouse near Taupo and is broadening into adjacent uses such as hydrogen production and gas cleaning.", "notes": "Icehouse Ventures backing is now strongly confirmed: NBR's funding article names Icehouse Ventures as an existing investor (alongside Flying Kiwi Angels), and both CB Insights and PitchBook list it among Hot Lime Labs' investors. Other named/associated investors include Motion Capital, Climate Venture Capital Fund, Outset Ventures, Booster Innovation Fund, Punakaiki, Enterprise Angels, and Powerhouse Ventures (Powerhouse led an early-stage round). Stage marked \\"Seed\\" as a best estimate; aggregators label rounds inconsistently and a Pre-A/Series A was reportedly in progress as of 2024. Total-funding figures differ by source (CB Insights ~$2.43m vs PitchBook ~$5.79m). This is the Lower Hutt, NZ greenhouse-CO2 company at 68 Gracefield Road, Gracefield; distinct from any same-named foreign entity.", "stage": "Seed", "status": "active", "founded": "2017", "sources": ["https://hotlimelabs.com/about-us/", "https://hotlimelabs.com/technology/", "https://www.nbr.co.nz/tech/agritech-firm-hot-lime-labs-raises-4m-for-expansion/", "https://www.auckland.ac.nz/en/news/2024/10/04/business-heating-up-hot-lime-labs.html", "https://www.cbinsights.com/company/hot-lime-labs", "https://pitchbook.com/profiles/company/225542-44", "https://www.powerhouse-ventures.co.nz/news/powerhouse-leads-successful-early-stage-investment-for-hot-lime-labs", "https://www.callaghaninnovation.govt.nz/stories/hot-lime-labs-putting-the-green-in-greenhouses-with-clean-carbon/", "https://kiwinet.org.nz/SuccessStories/HotLimeLabs"], "website": "https://hotlimelabs.com", "industry": "Cleantech / Agritech", "confidence": "high", "verifyNote": "Both founders independently confirmed by the company's own About page (Tijs Robinson – Co-founder, CEO & Director; Vlatko Materic – Co-founder & CTO), with backgrounds corroborated by Callaghan Innovation, KiwiNet and Powerhouse Ventures sources. Reordered founders to put the originating CTO first and dropped the honorific \\"Dr\\" prefix on display names while keeping the verified PhD detail in bio. Icehouse Ventures backing UPGRADED from a hedged note to firmly confirmed (NBR explicitly names Icehouse as an existing investor; CB Insights and PitchBook concur), so removed the draft caveat implying it was only inferred. Clarified the NZ$4m round was announced August 2023. Added that the greenhouse product is now commercialised via partner Aplenty (per the company site). Dropped \\"biochar\\" from the description as it could not be confirmed on current company materials (only hydrogen and gas cleaning are listed); biochar appears only in older coverage. Founders' exact LinkedIn URLs could not be opened directly (LinkedIn HTTP 999), but the Vlatko Materic URL was surfaced verbatim in search with the matching \\"Founder & CTO of Hot Lime Labs\\" title.", "statusDetail": "Active; operated a commercial demonstration CO2 capture unit at the Gourmet Mokai greenhouse (Taupo) and, per 2024 reporting, was finalising a Pre-A raise ahead of a planned Series A. Greenhouse product commercialised via partner Aplenty.", "verifyVerdict": "corrected", "fundingNotable": "Raised a NZ$4m over-subscribed round announced August 2023, reportedly valuing the company at ~NZ$20m. PitchBook reports ~NZ$5.79m total raised across multiple rounds (CB Insights lists ~US/NZ$2.43m on a partial view).", "icehouseBacked": "confirmed"}	community:icehouse-ventures	\N	2026-05-31 08:57:29.572	2026-06-01 01:42:36.222	Startup
org:iv-dennison-technologies	organization	Dennisson Technologies	NZ deep-tech startup building AURAFLEX, a light-activated artificial muscle (photonic actuator)	Auckland, New Zealand	https://www.dennisson.tech/	{"advanced materials","artificial muscle",actuators,robotics,wearables,"Icehouse Portfolio"}	\N	{"bio": "Dennisson Technologies is an Auckland, New Zealand advanced-materials deep-tech startup developing AURAFLEX, a \\"photonic muscle\\" — a light-activated artificial muscle made of polymer layers that contracts when exposed to light rather than using electricity or motors. The company positions the technology as a lightweight, low-cost, silent actuator for VR haptics, soft robotics, wearables, prosthetics, humanoid robotics and assistive exosuits. It was co-founded in 2020 by husband-and-wife engineers Anvil Serg Bañez and Kimberly Bañez and named after Anvil's father, Dennis, whose childhood polio inspired the mission. In 2023 it raised NZ$1.6m in a round led by Icehouse Ventures and Outset Ventures, and it exhibited at CES 2025.", "notes": "Correct legal/brand spelling is \\"Dennisson\\" (double-s), not \\"Dennison\\" as supplied; product brand is AURAFLEX/AuraFlex. Do NOT confuse with US firm Avery Dennison or other same-named entities. Icehouse Ventures backing independently confirmed (Icehouse listed among lead investors; also appears in CB Insights/PitchBook investor lists). Founder CEO/CTO roles match the company's own About page and BusinessDesk. Michael Blackhurst (COO) and Steve Leftly (CCO) are senior non-founder hires. NZBN 9429048301718 (DENNISSON TECHNOLOGIES LIMITED) exists per NZ Companies Office / Company Hub.", "stage": "Seed", "status": "active", "founded": "2020", "sources": ["https://www.dennisson.tech/about", "https://www.startmate.com/writing/dennisson-anvil-kimberly-banez-redefining-limits-human-capability", "https://outset.ventures/auraflex-dennisson-leap-in-wearable-tech/", "https://www.icehouseventures.co.nz/portfolio", "https://businessdesk.co.nz/article/health/local-tech-startup-flexes-artificial-muscle", "https://evencapital.nz/even-capital-backs-dennission-technologies-to-create-artificial-muscle/", "https://www.cbinsights.com/company/dennisson", "https://exhibitors.ces.tech/8_0/exhibitor/exhibitor-details.cfm?exhid=001Pp00000jn537IAA", "https://www.linkedin.com/in/anvil-serg-ba%C3%B1ez-2139a61a9/", "https://keanewzealand.com/personal-mission-fuels-kiwi-tech-company/"], "website": "https://www.dennisson.tech/", "industry": "Advanced Materials / Deep Tech", "confidence": "high", "verifyNote": "Independently confirmed via fresh searches: both founders are real with correct names and CEO/CTO roles (company About page, Startmate, BusinessDesk); Anvil's Waikato/RNZAF/DTA background corroborated; founded 2020 in Auckland; AURAFLEX light-activated artificial muscle accurate; NZ$1.6m 2023 round led by Icehouse Ventures and Outset Ventures with Even Capital and other NZ angels; CES 2025 exhibitor confirmed. No facts required correction. Minor caveats: the primary funding PDF and individual founder LinkedIn pages could not be fetched directly (binary/access limits), so the full investor list (Startmate/K1W1/AngelHQ/Edale Capital) and Kimberly's LinkedIn URL rely on the draft plus secondary corroboration rather than a primary fetch. Input name spelling \\"Dennison\\" corrected to \\"Dennisson\\".", "statusDetail": "Active; still developing/commercializing the AURAFLEX photonic muscle platform (exhibited at CES 2025).", "verifyVerdict": "confirmed", "fundingNotable": "Raised NZ$1.6m (Jan 2023) led by Icehouse Ventures and Outset Ventures; round also included Even Capital and other NZ angel investors (draft additionally lists Startmate, K1W1, AngelHQ and Edale Capital). Earlier supported via Startmate accelerator.", "icehouseBacked": "confirmed"}	community:icehouse-ventures	\N	2026-05-31 08:57:29.572	2026-06-01 01:42:36.222	Startup
org:iv-geo40	organization	Geo40 Limited	NZ cleantech recovering silica (and now lithium) sustainably from geothermal brine	Taupo, New Zealand	https://geo40.com/	{geothermal,silica,"lithium extraction",cleantech,"advanced materials","Icehouse Portfolio"}	\N	{"bio": "Geo40 is a New Zealand technology development and operating company that sustainably recovers valuable minerals from geothermal fluids, and is the first to commercialise large-scale extraction of colloidal silica from geothermal brine. Its Northern Plant at the Ohaaki geothermal site (commissioned 2021) has a 6,700 tpa silica nameplate capacity, producing low-carbon silica products. The company is extending its technology to direct lithium extraction (DLE), with a pre-commercial demonstration plant funded in 2025 for North American deployment, and is exploring recovery of other minerals such as boron and caesium. Founded in 2010 as Environmetals Limited, it rebranded to Geo40 in 2017.", "notes": "This is the NZ geothermal-minerals company at geo40.com (Geo40 Limited), not any same-named foreign entity. Founders: Geo40's own history page names only Mike O'Sullivan and Matt Sutcliffe; The Spinoff (2020) independently names a third co-founder, Adam Peren ('fellow founders Matt Sutcliffe and Adam Peren'), and search snippets from the history page corroborate all three plus Peren's later board role/2018 departure — all three retained. John Worth is the current CEO & Managing Director and is NOT a founder; he was added to the leadership later. HQ functionally Taupo (Ohaaki plant); the Icehouse job-board listing also cites Auckland and Waikato sites. PitchBook/Crunchbase pages return 403 and could not be fetched directly; the ~NZ$45.6M total and the '24 investors including Icehouse Ventures' claim come from search-result snippets, not a fetched primary page.", "stage": "Late-stage venture / growth (no single named round; ~NZ$45.6M+ raised across many investors and government funds, per PitchBook)", "status": "active", "founded": "2010", "sources": ["https://geo40.com/about/history-of-geo40/", "https://geo40.com/About/Our-People/", "https://geo40.com/investors/", "https://geo40.com/who-we-are/", "https://thespinoff.co.nz/business/04-11-2020/could-nzs-geothermal-resources-solve-the-worlds-lithium-mining-woes", "https://jobs.icehouseventures.co.nz/companies/geo40", "https://pitchbook.com/profiles/company/279544-15", "https://www.nbr.co.nz/your-business/geo40-raising-around-10m-for-next-stage-of-lithium-journey/", "https://www.vision-blue.com/portfolio/Geo-40/", "https://www.thinkgeoenergy.com/geo40-secures-5-4m-for-geothermal-lithium-extraction-pilot/", "https://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/news/business/the-ultra-high-net-worth-investor-spending-big-in-new-zealand/", "https://www.baseloadcap.com/homegrown-critical-minerals-is-that-possible-john-worth-ceo-geo40/"], "website": "https://geo40.com/", "industry": "Advanced Materials / Clean Technology (geothermal mineral extraction)", "confidence": "high", "verifyNote": "Independently re-verified every claim with fresh searches; the draft was substantially accurate. Confirmed via primary sources: 2010 founding as Environmetals, 2017 rebrand, Northern Plant 2021, active status, John Worth as CEO/MD (not a founder), and the Vision Blue/Tembo/Pacific Channel/Crown Regional shareholders on Geo40's own top-10 list. Founder trio (O'Sullivan, Sutcliffe, Peren) confirmed via The Spinoff; note Geo40's current history page lists only the first two, so Peren rests on media corroboration. Softened O'Sullivan's role from 'COO' to 'Co-founder' (the COO title comes only from a 2020 article; he is not on the current leadership page) and removed his unverified LinkedIn URL since it could not be independently confirmed. Icehouse backing kept as 'likely': only PitchBook (via snippet) lists Icehouse among ~24 investors; Geo40's own 31-May-2025 top-10 shareholder list does not name Icehouse, and the newstalkzb article does not causally tie Icehouse to Geo40 — consistent with a small position among the 350+ investors. Raised confidence to high overall given strong primary-source corroboration of most fields.", "statusDetail": "Active and operating. Northern Plant commissioned 2021; reported consecutive strong silica sales quarters heading toward silica-plant profitability; lithium pre-commercial demonstration plant funded and under construction in 2025 for North American deployment. CEO & Managing Director John Worth (not a founder).", "verifyVerdict": "confirmed", "fundingNotable": "Reported ~NZ$45.6M total raised (per PitchBook search snippet). Backers named on Geo40's own top-10 shareholder list (31 May 2025) include Vision Blue Resources, Tembo Capital, Crown Regional Holdings, and Pacific Channel; co-founder Michael O'Sullivan and director Peter Bradford also appear. Other widely reported backers include Baseload Capital (linked to Bill Gates' Breakthrough Energy Ventures) and government funding (Provincial Growth Fund / Kanoa). Company states it is supported by 350+ investors.", "icehouseBacked": "likely"}	community:icehouse-ventures	\N	2026-05-31 08:57:29.572	2026-06-01 01:42:36.222	Startup
org:iv-goodworld	organization	Goodworld	Social-impact software for fundraising, workplace giving and CSR, founded by Kiwi Dale Pfeifer.	Washington, DC, USA	https://goodworldnow.com/	{"social impact",fintech,"charitable giving","corporate social responsibility","workplace engagement","Icehouse Portfolio"}	\N	{"bio": "Goodworld is a Washington, DC-based social-impact technology platform that helps companies and nonprofits run fundraising, employee giving, volunteering and engagement campaigns. It first gained attention in 2014-2015 with \\"#donate\\" technology that let people give to charities by typing a hashtag on Facebook and Twitter, then expanded into a broader corporate-giving and workplace-engagement platform used by brands including Mastercard, Citi and Lyft. It was founded by New Zealand-born (Invercargill) Dale Nirvani Pfeifer with co-founder John Gossart. Goodworld is listed in Icehouse Ventures' New Zealand portfolio, reflecting that VC's practice of backing Kiwi founders building globally.", "notes": "Disambiguation: there is one relevant \\"Goodworld\\" here, but it is easily misread. The company is headquartered in Washington, DC (USA) yet was founded by Invercargill-born New Zealander Dale Nirvani Pfeifer, which is why Icehouse Ventures (a NZ VC backing Kiwi founders building globally) lists it. It operates under two domains that are the same company: the original goodworld.me and the current goodworldnow.com. Goodworld's own about page lists Dale Pfeifer as \\"Founder & CEO\\" and a co-founder \\"John\\" as \\"Co-Founder & COO\\"; some third-party databases (Crunchbase/ZoomInfo) list Gossart's title variously as CEO/CFO over time. Founding year is cited as 2014 (founder's stated start / company about page) and sometimes 2015 (US incorporation / seed-round coverage).", "stage": "Seed", "status": "active", "founded": "2014", "sources": ["https://www.icehouseventures.co.nz/portfolio", "https://goodworldnow.com/", "https://goodworld.me/about", "https://www.nzedge.com/news/startup-goodworld-raises-1-65m-series-seed-round-donate-technology/", "https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/on-small-business/wp/2015/07/07/start-up-that-automates-charitable-donation-raises-1-65-million-from-investors/", "https://www.finsmes.com/2015/07/goodworld-secures-1-65m-in-seed-funding.html", "https://www.mindfood.com/article/smart-thinker-dale-nirvani-pfeifer-from-goodworld/", "https://www.stuff.co.nz/southland-times/news/97636056/invercargills-dale-nirvani-pfeifer-named-as-woman-to-watch-in-washington", "https://givingcompass.org/press-release/dale-pfeifer-ceo", "https://beyondtheuniform.org/blog/btu-109-20-years-in-the-army-selling-a-startup-to-mercedes-and-co-founding-goodworld-john-gossart", "https://www.crunchbase.com/person/john-gossart"], "website": "https://goodworldnow.com/", "industry": "Software / Fintech-for-good (social impact, corporate giving)", "confidence": "high", "verifyNote": "Both founders independently confirmed: Goodworld's own about page (goodworld.me/about) shows Dale Nirvani Pfeifer as \\"Founder & CEO\\" and a co-founder \\"John\\" as \\"Co-Founder & COO,\\" matching the draft. The $1.65M seed (led by Nyca Partners, July 2015, with Chris Liddell as an angel), the #donate hashtag origin, Mastercard/Citi/Lyft customers, Pfeifer's NZ/Invercargill origin, and her May 2023 move to Giving Compass CEO are all corroborated by NZEDGE, Washington Post, FinSMEs, Stuff and Giving Compass. Goodworld IS listed in Icehouse Ventures' portfolio (confirmed via direct fetch of the portfolio page) — but no specific Icehouse round, amount or date is publicly disclosed and Goodworld's own investor materials do not name Icehouse, so I downgraded icehouseBacked from \\"confirmed\\" to \\"likely.\\" Raised overall confidence to high given the strong multi-source corroboration. Could not independently confirm the precise prior-education details (e.g., specific Massey degree titles) or the \\"Centre for the Study of Leadership\\" wording, 